<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/friday-11-sep-2026-09-00-48-gradient-muted-more-space-1789215245472-1faaae1d4a.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<aside class="pull-quote aligncenter" id="aside2"><blockquote>
<p>Invention, it must be humbly admitted, does not consist in creating out of void, but out of chaos.</p>
<p>— Mary Shelley, introduction to <em>Frankenstein</em> (1831)</p>
</blockquote>
</aside><p id="p3">Every OS update has a story to tell. With macOS 27 Golden Gate, that story captures a moment when Apple is faced with multiple challenges, many of which are tied to the rise of large language models.</p>
<p id="p4">One of those challenges is obvious. Apple was caught off guard by chatbots, overpromised a response at WWDC in 2024, and has been playing catch-up ever since.</p>
<p id="p5">But this isn’t simply a story of missteps or wasted opportunities. There’s more to it than that.</p>
<aside class="aside aligncenter aside-narrow" id="aside6"><h3 class="pre-title" id="supported-by">Supported By</h3>
<h2 id="inoreader">Inoreader</h2>

<p></p><center><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/inoreader-icon-1080-1789344309643-a6bb47a451.png" width="180" alt="" style="display:block; margin:20px auto; border-radius:22.5%;"></center>
<p><strong><a href="https://www.inoreader.com/?utm_source=macstories&amp;utm_medium=tagline&amp;utm_campaign=macos_review" rel="noopener noreferrer noopener noreferrer">Inoreader:</a></strong> Build your own newsfeed and cut through the noise.</p>
</aside><p id="p7"><!--more--></p>
<p id="p8">Two years before ChatGPT took the world by storm and began <a href="https://www.investing.com/news/stock-market-news/chatgpt-app-hits-1-billion-monthly-active-users-in-record-time-data-shows-4723123" rel="noopener noreferrer">its march to 1 billion monthly users</a>, Apple released <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2020/11/apple-unleashes-m1/" rel="noopener noreferrer">the first Apple silicon Macs</a>. Whether intentional or a happy coincidence, Apple silicon’s unified memory architecture <a href="https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2025/298/" rel="noopener noreferrer">fits like a glove</a> for running and fine-tuning LLMs.</p>
<p id="p9">macOS’ UNIX roots make it a great fit for AI development, too. From working with Python to coding with agents in Terminal, the Mac offers a familiar command-line environment for developers working with AI.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-84645-am2x-1789217269204-99b549e173.png" alt="Claude Code at work on a Mac app."><p class="image-caption">Claude Code at work on a Mac app.</p></div>
<p id="p11">Apple’s strength in these areas represents a huge opportunity for it and the Mac in the AI era. However, it’s that very industry that’s also fueling the challenges Apple faces.</p>
<p id="p12">Developers in the AI industry and power users setting up <a href="https://openclaw.ai/" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenClaw</a> servers may have juiced demand for MacBook Pros and Mac minis, but data centers have made it hard for Apple to meet the demand for Macs and hold prices steady. <a href="https://www.wired.com/story/mac-mini-availability/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wait times for some Macs</a> stretched into weeks and months this summer, and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/06/17/apple-increasing-prices/" rel="noopener noreferrer">prices have risen</a>. Still, Apple has continued to barrel ahead with new models, most recently updating <a href="https://www.macstories.net/news/apple-announces-new-m6-and-m5-pro-mac-minis-along-with-m5-max-and-m5-ultra-mac-studios/" rel="noopener noreferrer">the Mac Studio and Mac mini</a>.</p>
<p id="p13">The AI industry has also been a drain on Apple’s talent pool, attracting <a href="https://www.axios.com/2026/08/06/openai-apple-motion-to-dismiss" rel="noopener noreferrer">hundreds of employees</a> from across the company. Those departures are the backdrop to Apple’s high-profile <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jul/10/apple-sues-openai-trade-secrets" rel="noopener noreferrer">trade secrets lawsuit against OpenAI</a>, which involves former employees now working on AI-focused hardware. The result is a messy, turbulent era for Apple and the entire tech industry.</p>
<p id="p14">At the same time, macOS is completing its transition to Apple silicon by <a href="https://9to5mac.com/2026/09/01/apple-tells-mac-app-store-developers-they-can-now-drop-intel-support/" rel="noopener noreferrer">dropping support for Intel-based Macs in Golden Gate</a>. It’s a big milestone, but one that pales in comparison to the <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/04/tim-cook-to-become-apple-executive-chairman-john-ternus-to-become-apple-ceo/" rel="noopener noreferrer">just-completed CEO handoff from Tim Cook to John Ternus</a>.</p>
<p id="p15">Against that unsettled backdrop, macOS 27 Golden Gate has a job to do. It needs to finish the transitions Apple has already begun by:</p>
<ul id="ul16"><li>cleaning up loose ends from the transition to Apple silicon,</li>
<li>making good on Apple Intelligence promises of 2024, and</li>
<li>tightening up the Liquid Glass design refresh that began with macOS Tahoe.</li>
</ul><p id="p17">There are many other changes to system apps and other parts of macOS that I’ll explore in this review, too. But these three pillars invite us to evaluate how well Golden Gate builds on Apple silicon’s already solid foundation, delivers on Apple’s 2024 AI promises, and improves the everyday experience of using a Mac.</p>
<p id="p18">With that, let’s dig into the details.</p>
<p id="p19"><!--more--></p>
<p id="p20"><a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/macos-27-the-macstories-review/" rel="noopener noreferrer">View this on MacStories.</a></p>
<p id="p21">[table_of_contents]</p>
<p id="p22"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h2 id="design">Design</h2>
<p id="p23">Of the three transitions that shape macOS 27, the Liquid Glass design language is the most recent. <a href="https://www.macstories.net/links/opinionated-design-early-reactions-to-apples-liquid-glass-design-language/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Announced at WWDC in 2025</a>, the change drew strong reactions from early users and beta testers. Despite continued criticism in some quarters and vows not to upgrade until Tahoe’s design was “fixed,” I like Liquid Glass, as do most people I’ve talked to about it in my day-to-day life.</p>
<p id="p24">However, liking Liquid Glass is not the same as feeling that Apple nailed it with Tahoe. It didn’t. Tahoe’s design felt incomplete. It was as though there had been an all-hands meeting of Apple’s software designers who were given a detailed briefing on how to implement Liquid Glass but then they never talked again. Some apps took an aggressive Liquid Glass approach similar to iOS 26, while other apps felt like they’d barely been touched. In fact, there were even inconsistencies between how Liquid Glass was applied to <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/macos-26-tahoe-the-macstories-review/2/#windows-sidebars-and-toolbars" rel="noopener noreferrer">different view modes in Finder</a>.</p>
<p id="p25">Golden Gate addresses those issues with a more cohesive, readable experience. It also gives users control over transparency, addressing one of the core criticisms of Liquid Glass.</p>
<h3 id="icons">Icons</h3>
<p id="p26">One of the most subtle changes to Liquid Glass is the way it diffuses light. The best place to inspect the difference is by looking at the icons side by side. The edges are darker, and the highlights are brighter, adding a greater sense of depth, while avoiding the softness and blurriness of Tahoe icons. The result is that UI elements are sharper and more readable, which is an accessibility and usability win.</p>
<p id="p27">Liquid Glass’ light diffusion update isn’t limited to icons, but because it’s one of the best ways to visualize the change, I’ve collected a few examples that show the change off especially well.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/08/29/codex-image-aug-29-2026-03-15-01-pm-1788030942659-4f50a9e2c2.png" alt="Examples of the evolution of Mac system app icons from Sequoia through two Liquid Glass iterations."><p class="image-caption">Examples of the evolution of Mac system app icons from Sequoia through two Liquid Glass iterations.</p></div>
<p id="p29">I like the refinement to Apple’s system app icons. What once made my screen look unnaturally soft and mushy is now crisp.</p>
<p id="p30">Alongside these changes, many icons have received small tweaks, such as <a href="https://basicappleguy.com/basicappleblog/macos-golden-gate-icon-comparison" rel="noopener noreferrer">Finder’s rounder nose</a>. There’s also one wholly new icon and several material redesigns.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/08/29/download-1788032441748-e2f86581b9.png" alt="The new Siri icon alongside redesigned icons for Preview, Chess, Finder, Image Playground, and iPhone Mirroring."><p class="image-caption">The new Siri icon alongside redesigned icons for Preview, Chess, Finder, Image Playground, and iPhone Mirroring.</p></div>
<p id="p32">What Golden Gate does not resolve is the so-called “Squircle Jail” controversy introduced by macOS 26. Starting with Tahoe, Apple mandated that all icon glyphs appear within the bounds of their background tile. Icons that violate the new restriction usually end up shrunken inside a generic “squircle jail” tile, which doesn’t look good.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/codex-image-sep-12-2026-09-19-45-am-1789219272287-a570aab5c0.png" alt='Examples of "squircle jail."'><p class="image-caption">Examples of “squircle jail.”</p></div>
<p id="p34">During the macOS 27 beta period, <a href="https://weblog.rogueamoeba.com/2026/06/26/free-the-icons/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Paul Kafasis</a> and <a href="https://daringfireball.net/2026/07/eliminate_app_icon_squircle_jail" rel="noopener noreferrer">John Gruber</a> called for Apple to eliminate its icon rule, while <a href="https://tyler.io/2026/07/05/escape-from-squircle-jail/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Tyler Hall</a> demonstrated a workaround. Nevertheless, “squircle jail” endures.</p>
<p id="p35">I can see both sides of this debate. On the one hand, as Kafasis argues, the uniform shapes Apple has imposed make it harder for people with color vision deficiencies to distinguish one icon from another. Former Apple designer <a href="https://rogerwong.me/2026/07/macos-squircle-icons" rel="noopener noreferrer">Roger Wong</a> takes a different tack, arguing, based on older Mac icons, that gaps in irregularly shaped icons can cause users to miss their click targets. Both arguments go to icon functionality and both have their merits.</p>
<p id="p36">On balance, I’m fine with the direction Apple has taken its icons. They mirror the design of icons on the iPhone and iPad, and there’s value in consistency for users who don’t think as deeply about icons as some Apple nerds. We’ve been trained through habit to expect that <a href="https://parakeet.co/blog/the-shape-of-apps/" rel="noopener noreferrer">a squircle is an app</a>. I also think Wong’s argument takes on even greater significance if the rumors that Apple is working on <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-10-16/apple-readies-high-end-macbook-pro-with-touch-hole-punch-screen" rel="noopener noreferrer">a touchscreen Mac</a> are true.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/download-1789223230153-5996c7aeb0.png" alt="Golden Gate takes a more restrained approach to icons in menus."><p class="image-caption">Golden Gate takes a more restrained approach to icons in menus.</p></div>
<p id="p38">Finally, the proliferation of SF Symbols in app menus has been reversed, too. You’ll see fewer in system apps, and Apple’s <a href="https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/menus#Icons" rel="noopener noreferrer">Human Interface Guidelines</a> have been revised to advise third-party developers to use the icons “sparingly and with purpose.” Also appearing in the menu bar is a new Ethernet icon that replaces the Wi-Fi icon when your Mac is wired to a network.</p>
<h3 id="transparency-and-toolbars">Transparency and Toolbars</h3>
<p id="p39">Alright, enough about icons. It’s time to think about transparency. Apple’s designers have significantly improved the readability of Liquid Glass elements throughout the system and given users a tool to tweak transparency to suit their own tastes.</p>
<p id="p40">I don’t envy Apple’s designers who have to design a UI that works with any app whether it exists today or someday in the future. The first iteration of Liquid Glass worked well with some apps, but fell down in spots, such as when the background of an app was busy and unpredictable. Music with its colorful album art and Photos with its views of photos of literally anything someone might take a picture of are both great examples. As you scrolled through music or photos, both apps’ toolbars often seemed to disappear or become unreadable as colors passed beneath the toolbars. Text-heavy apps like Finder presented a similar problem, sometimes causing the text of scrolling lists of files and icons to compete with the toolbar’s content.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/liquid-glass-slider-comparison-1789219103054-3a50da3f6a.png" alt="Appearance settings now include a transparency slider and a preview of its effect."><p class="image-caption">Appearance settings now include a transparency slider and a preview of its effect.</p></div>
<p id="p42">macOS Golden Gate solves this with toolbars that diffuse the content beneath them better at the OS’s default setting. That default is squarely at the midpoint of a slider found near the top of the Appearance section of System Settings. Move the slider’s indicator to the left and more of the background will bleed through toolbars, and move it to the right and the background frosts over to a nearly solid color. The setting is accompanied by a handy preview that shows the effect that each point along the spectrum has.</p>
<p id="p43">A great example of the change is Photos, which has regained a toolbar structure that separates buttons from the content scrolling behind them. It’s a welcome change to an app that struggled with a more transparent Liquid Glass approach.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/photos-toolbar-tahoe-golden-gate-1789224483885-d740064450.png" alt="Photos’ updated toolbar gives its controls a clearer separation from the images that scroll underneath it."><p class="image-caption">Photos’ updated toolbar gives its controls a clearer separation from the images that scroll underneath it.</p></div>
<p id="p45">Apple’s solution is a good one. It would have been simple enough to give users a slider and be done with the transparency controversy, but instead its designers took the time to improve the default, too. The new default strikes a good balance between transparency and opacity that I expect most users will never feel the need to change. In contrast, I’ve always liked Liquid Glass’ transparency, so I set it all the way to the left where things are most transparent and never looked back.</p>
<p id="p46">Interestingly, I did the same thing on the iPhone and eventually dialed the Liquid Glass back a little. It may be the smaller screen or the fact that I use my iPhone in the bright North Carolina sun so often, but on the Mac, full transparency looks less extreme to me.</p>
<p id="p47">Toolbars also benefit from a more unified treatment. With Golden Gate, a continuous bar appears beneath controls automatically when content scrolls under them.</p>
<p id="p48"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h3 id="sidebars-corner-radii-and-drop-shadows">Sidebars, Corner Radii, and Drop Shadows</h3>
<p id="p49">macOS Tahoe turned sidebars into glass-like slabs that sat on top of a window. I liked the layered look, but many others didn’t. Plus, when one window was obscured by others, it was too hard to tell if what you saw poking out was two windows sitting on top of each other and slightly offset or a sidebar.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/finder-sidebars-tahoe-golden-gate-1789225084243-d4ced91963.png" alt="Sidebars extend to the edges of windows, and deeper shadows make overlapping windows easier to pick out."><p class="image-caption">Sidebars extend to the edges of windows, and deeper shadows make overlapping windows easier to pick out.</p></div>
<p id="p51">The fix is to extend sidebars to the top, bottom, and side to which they’re anchored. The sidebars retain their glassy look and icons <a href="https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2026/102/" rel="noopener noreferrer">have regained their color</a>, but the layering effect is gone. I can’t help but feel like something was lost in the update, but from a practical standpoint, it’s a usability win.</p>
<p id="p52">Golden Gate also brings more consistent corner radii to system windows. I honestly never noticed the difference between window corners, but it bothered some users who will no longer be bothered, which is a plus.</p>
<p id="p53">Finally, there was something about Liquid Glass that could make it hard to distinguish one window from another. I experienced this with Tahoe myself, especially in Dark Mode. macOS Golden Gate has more pronounced drop shadows, which become the new normal very quickly, but do a better job of defining the separation between windows.</p>
<h3 id="wallpapers">Wallpapers</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/codex-image-sep-12-2026-11-27-00-am-1789226873632-6d170bf28f.png" alt="Golden Gate’s abstract curves (top) and the Golden Gate Sunset wallpapers (bottom)."><p class="image-caption">Golden Gate’s abstract curves (top) and the Golden Gate Sunset wallpapers (bottom).</p></div>
<p id="p55">As always, this year’s macOS update comes with a beautiful set of wallpapers including Golden Gate, a dynamic golden wallpaper with sweeping curves that vaguely resemble the number “27,” and Golden Gate Sunset, a shot of the Golden Gate Bridge with an animated screen saver that’s mesmerizing to watch.</p>
<p id="p56">This year, the Mac’s stylized wallpaper also appears on other Apple devices, bringing <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/06/09/apple-unifies-wallpapers-for-the-first-time/" rel="noopener noreferrer">a shared look across platforms</a>.</p>
<h3 id="other-tidbits">Other Tidbits</h3>
<p id="p57">We’re living through <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/the-utility-app-flood-wont-last/" rel="noopener noreferrer">a menu bar app explosion</a> thanks to AI coding agents, making it harder than ever to manage your menu bar. That’s especially true on Mac laptops with a notch. It really doesn’t take much to fill up the available space and end up with icons hidden by the notch or the tail end of an app’s menus.</p>
<p id="p58">There are third-party apps like <a href="https://icemenubar.app/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ice</a> that will manage your menu bar for you, but new macOS releases can break them. That’s why on my MacBook Pro, I’ve historically aggressively managed my menu bar instead. But even then, it can be hard to avoid hitting the notch.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/codex-image-sep-12-2026-11-33-20-am-1789227225982-d4ab5e2cf2.png" alt="The double-arrow icon reveals menu bar icons hidden by the notch or an app’s menu system."><p class="image-caption">The double-arrow icon reveals menu bar icons hidden by the notch or an app’s menu system.</p></div>
<p id="p60">With macOS Golden Gate, Apple has added an overflow control in the form of a double arrow that appears when the notch or menu system is covering menu bar items. Click it and the hidden menu bar items appear to the left of the notch temporarily, so you can interact with them. This is especially helpful if you use all of the available space up to the notch and a new app installs a menu bar item that you want to move where you can see it, but it’s been hidden.</p>
<aside class="pull-quote aligncenter" id="aside61"><blockquote>
<p>The Mac isn’t a static hardware platform, and its OS shouldn’t be either.</p>
</blockquote>
</aside><p id="p62">I like what Apple has done with Liquid Glass in Golden Gate a lot. My main reservations last year were the inconsistent implementation and poor legibility in apps like Photos and Music. The result is more coherent and usable, though whether the update is enough to satisfy Liquid Glass’ harshest critics remains to be seen.</p>
<p id="p63">The shape of icons matters, but it isn’t the design change that affects my use of the Mac the most. Clearer toolbars, windows that are easier to distinguish, and consistent controls have had a much more substantial impact.</p>
<p id="p64">More importantly, though, I appreciate seeing macOS’ design evolve alongside its hardware and the new tasks they’re called on to do. The Mac isn’t a static hardware platform, and its OS shouldn’t be either. I’ll miss the flourish of icons that escape their bounds, but if it makes it easier for more people to move from the iPhone, which is a far larger platform, to the Mac, that’s great. Software is never finished. macOS Tahoe’s design pointed the Mac in the right direction, but was a big enough departure from the past that it had some rough edges. Golden Gate addresses my biggest complaints with Tahoe and has been a pleasure to use all summer.</p>
<p id="p65"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h2 id="siri-ai-and-apple-intelligence">Siri AI and Apple Intelligence</h2>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/apple-os-27-updates-260608-1789229176856-d8e5d77794.jpg" alt="Apple Intelligence spans all of Apple's platforms. Source: Apple."><p class="image-caption">Apple Intelligence spans all of Apple’s platforms. Source: Apple.</p></div>
<p id="p67">The Siri app may be the marquee update of Golden Gate, but Apple Intelligence touches nearly every surface of the OS this year. In fact, I wouldn’t be surprised if, as soon as next year, features that integrate some sort of AI are still categorized under the umbrella term “Apple Intelligence” but are no longer treated as the standalone category that they have been for the past few years.</p>
<p id="p68">I say that because what’s striking this year is that everything about Apple Intelligence feels better integrated into macOS. Siri is perhaps the best example of all. What was once a voice assistant remains a voice assistant, but it has its own app now, too. Plus, Siri can be invoked from Spotlight, the Services menu, or simply by highlighting text and querying it with the “Ask Siri” contextual popup. It’s the sort of tight integration that Apple is known for, and its absence made some past iterations of Apple Intelligence feel bolted on.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-120741-pm2x-1789229297255-ba7389b063.png" alt="Siri is embedded throughout macOS 27, including in Spotlight."><p class="image-caption">Siri is embedded throughout macOS 27, including in Spotlight.</p></div>
<p id="p70">Before diving into the new Siri app, it’s worth keeping in mind that Siri AI is <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/06/apple-introduces-siri-ai-a-profoundly-more-capable-and-personal-assistant/" rel="noopener noreferrer">launching as a beta</a> on Apple’s platforms and is only available in English. Apple has also restricted Siri AI’s use, <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/09/apple-unveils-iphone-duo/" rel="noopener noreferrer">prohibiting kids under 13</a> from using it, and it’s not available in the EU on the iPhone, iPad, and Apple Watch, but <em>is</em> available on the Mac. The new AI features <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/06/apple-introduces-siri-ai-a-profoundly-more-capable-and-personal-assistant/" rel="noopener noreferrer">won’t be available in China initially</a> either.</p>
<p id="p71">What’s less clear is what limits Apple will put on Siri AI usage. Apple is offering enhanced access for now, but says cloud-based features are <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/127901" rel="noopener noreferrer">subject to usage limits</a> and expanded access will eventually be <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/09/apple-unveils-iphone-duo/" rel="noopener noreferrer">a paid feature</a>. The practical impact of these limits is currently unclear. That’s an important thing to keep in mind because my evaluation of Siri AI is as it exists today. Whether it’s worth paying for more than whatever the base usage winds up being will have to be evaluated another day.</p>
<p id="p72">I’ll get to all of the Apple Intelligence features you need to know about here and elsewhere in this review, but the best place to start is with the new Siri app because it’s undeniably the epicenter not just of Apple Intelligence, but all of macOS Golden Gate.</p>
<h3 id="the-siri-app">The Siri App</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-120958-pm2x-1789229423988-612f9fd04b.png" alt="The new Siri app."><p class="image-caption">The new Siri app.</p></div>
<p id="p74">What goes around comes around. <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Siri" rel="noopener noreferrer">Siri started life as a standalone app all the way back in 2010</a>. With the release of macOS 27 and Apple’s other OS updates, Siri is once again a separate app, while also remaining deeply integrated in other parts of macOS.</p>
<p id="p75">However, the new Siri app isn’t users’ only alternative. Siri’s new smarts have been baked into Spotlight, the Services menu, and individual system apps. That makes Siri more accessible than ever, lowering the friction of getting answers considerably, which is a big improvement over past Siri implementations.</p>
<p id="p76">But having Siri answer questions and having it take actions on your behalf are very different things. So, while you can ask Siri just about anything from anywhere, the results you’ll get will depend considerably on the information or actions you’re seeking. Siri’s world knowledge and ability to take actions at your direction are a big leap from the past. If asking world knowledge questions is your primary use case, I expect you’ll like the Siri update. However, if you’re already deep into tools like ChatGPT Work, <a href="https://www.macstories.net/news/openai-unveils-codex-superapp-update-with-computer-use-automations-built-in-browser-and-more/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Codex</a>, <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/hands-on-with-claude-dispatch-for-cowork/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Claude Cowork</a>, or <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/how-i-revived-my-decade-old-app-with-claude-code/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Claude Code</a> for automation or assisting you with typical office work, you’re going to hit friction points quickly. That’s why it’s worth exploring what Siri can and can’t do as you think about how it might fit into your everyday work.</p>
<p id="p77">There’s a lot of ground to cover with Siri, so let’s get started with the Siri app itself.</p>
<h3 id="the-app-and-how-it-works">The App and How It Works</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-120250-pm2x-1789228998071-8b13380ea4.png" alt="Asking Siri about a video in Safari."><p class="image-caption">Asking Siri about a video in Safari.</p></div>
<p id="p79">If you’ve used an app like <a href="https://chatgpt.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer">ChatGPT</a>, <a href="https://claude.ai/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Claude</a>, <a href="https://gemini.google.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Gemini</a>, or <a href="https://www.perplexity.ai/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Perplexity</a> before, you’ll know how to use the Siri app. In fact, it’s far simpler than those apps. The Siri app has a sidebar on the left that displays your past chat threads in reverse chronological order, syncing them among your devices via iCloud Sync. Threads can be displayed as a list or a grid, which shows off images and other rich media in a thread. You can also pin conversations, which then sit at the top of the list or grid regardless of how many other chats you begin, and delete past conversations individually or in batches by selecting multiple chats.</p>
<p id="p80">The main part of the Siri app’s window is where you chat with Siri AI, Apple’s updated assistant. From here you can type queries or speak them using Apple’s built-in dictation feature. You can also add files and images to a conversation, start a new conversation or search past conversations. There’s really not anything more to it than that. In fact, the Siri app doesn’t even have its own in-app settings. You’ll find those in System Settings, instead.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/08/cleanshot-2026-09-08-at-81559-am2x-1788869773054-52a3f943c7.png" alt="The Siri app's settings are part of System Settings' broader set of Siri settings."><p class="image-caption">The Siri app’s settings are part of System Settings’ broader set of Siri settings.</p></div>
<p id="p82">System Settings brings together settings for Siri the voice assistant and app. For the app, you’ll find options to:</p>
<ul id="ul83"><li>open the app to a recent or new conversation, </li>
<li>keep conversations for 30 days, a year, or forever, and</li>
<li>show in-app conversation previews of 1–5 lines.</li>
</ul><p id="p84">As for Siri’s answers, they’re generated by a combination of on-device and Private Cloud Compute models. The on-device model runs locally on your Mac and Private Cloud Compute (PCC) runs in the cloud. <a href="https://security.apple.com/blog/private-cloud-compute/" rel="noopener noreferrer">As Apple has explained</a>, only the data necessary to satisfy a request is sent to Private Cloud Compute, where it is inaccessible to Apple:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote85"><p>
  PCC deletes the user’s data after fulfilling the request, and no user data is retained in any form after the response is returned.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p86">Whether any particular answer is generated by an on-device model or PCC is not highlighted by Apple. That said, I’ve been happy with the speed of the Siri app’s responses. I’ve also tested PCC and tried the Foundation Models framework in a couple of apps I worked on over the summer and have been impressed with PCC’s speed in that context, too.</p>
<p id="p87"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h3 id="world-knowledge">World Knowledge</h3>
<p id="p88">Most of the people I know primarily use chatbots the way they used to use Google. They look up facts about the world around them from the hours that the local diner is open to the actor in a movie they’re watching on TV. It says a lot about what’s become of Google Search that a chatbot that sometimes gets facts wrong is preferable to searching the web, but that’s where we are in 2026, and I can’t really blame anyone who just wants an answer without a lot of ads and other nonsense.</p>
<p id="p89">If that’s how you’ve used chatbots like the free version of ChatGPT, I expect you’ll like the Siri app. It’s not perfect, but it’s good, and in my experience, Siri’s gotten better over the course of the summer.</p>
<p id="p90">In my <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/macos-27-golden-gate-the-macstories-public-beta-preview/" rel="noopener noreferrer">macOS 27 beta preview story</a>, I likened Siri to Google’s AI Overviews. Since then, Siri has improved, although the differences between services often come down to presentation and personal preference as much as the results themselves.</p>
<p id="p91">For instance, while I still find its suggestions for a vacation to Chicago a little uninspired, the results from Google AI Overviews and the free version of ChatGPT are about the same. The results of each service overlapped in places, and each set of results differed in certain ways, but all offered typical tourist destinations. Adding more detail about the kinds of things you’d want to do on a trip helped.</p>
<p id="p92">Your experience with Siri will depend a lot on what you want out of it too. I asked Siri for movies starring The Rock and got a list of four notable films and four additional bullet points expanding on the list. ChatGPT’s free service and Google AI Overviews both resulted in much longer lists of movies, but Google’s results were messy and OpenAI showed me an ad suggesting I book a hotel in Chicago based on my previous query. So, on balance, the Siri experience was the best of the three.</p>
<p id="p93">Historically, AI models have struggled to answer questions about events occurring after their training cutoff. Web search is used to help models answer questions beyond their training cutoff. <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/06/apple-introduces-siri-ai-a-profoundly-more-capable-and-personal-assistant/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apple says Siri supplements its world knowledge with the web</a>, too, but in my experience, the results aren’t as good as those from other chatbots. For example, Siri sometimes missed recent articles that Google AI Overviews and ChatGPT found.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-121153-pm2x-1789229577875-710ecdeccc.png" alt="Upcoming sports events expand to reveal more detail."><p class="image-caption">Upcoming sports events expand to reveal more detail.</p></div>
<p id="p95">One nice touch of the Siri app is that some results include rich content. For example, sports scores show the teams’ logos, and clicking a result expands it to reveal additional statistics. The presentation of scores and other information like biographies of musicians and images of landmarks seems pulled straight from rich results introduced in <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/macos-ventura-the-macstories-review/8/#spotlight" rel="noopener noreferrer">Spotlight with macOS Ventura in 2022</a>. However, unlike those results, which were ephemeral, disappearing when Spotlight was dismissed, these results become part of your conversation history in the Siri app, where they can be retrieved later with search. This goes to show the benefits of a dedicated Siri app instead of just relying on a voice assistant.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-121509-pm2x-1789229777788-b0645cc5cf.png" alt="Rich content in Siri results extends to other categories like movies and actors."><p class="image-caption">Rich content in Siri results extends to other categories like movies and actors.</p></div>
<p id="p97"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h3 id="take-action">Take Action!</h3>
<p id="p98">There’s more to Siri than just world knowledge, though. One of the headline features Apple promised at WWDC in 2024 was <a href="https://www.macstories.net/notes/trying-to-make-sense-of-the-rumored-gemini-powered-siri-overhaul/" rel="noopener noreferrer">the ability of Siri to take actions for you based on personal context</a> and draw on personal context to provide answers tailored to you.</p>
<p id="p99">Siri’s ability to perform actions on documents and other data in an app relies on <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents" rel="noopener noreferrer">App Intents</a>, the framework that supports features across Apple’s OSes, from Shortcuts to interactive widgets. In its simplest terms, App Intents lets developers describe their apps’ content and actions, which are like the nouns and verbs of a sentence. The nouns are things like a PDF document, photo, text message, or event, while the verbs are actions like send, edit, or open. Siri interprets a natural language request and connects it to the relevant content and available actions.</p>
<p id="p100">That makes it sound simple, but it’s not. There’s a lot that can go wrong from request to action, and for a lot of the summer it did. Siri often failed, and few actions were available besides ones for built-in system apps, but that’s changed.</p>
<p id="p101">In the closing weeks of the summer beta season, Siri’s ability to take actions consistently and in apps other than system apps has really come together. That’s not to say it’s perfect or that every app I’d like to see adopt App Intents has, but I’m far more optimistic about Siri taking actions in apps than I was in June.</p>
<p id="p102">For example, I have a trip coming up at the end of the month. I asked Siri to remind me what day I’m leaving. It found the email confirming my flight and gave me the date and relevant flight information. Then I asked Siri if I had any plans while I was away and it found a different email confirming that I’m attending OpenAI’s DevDay. Finally, I asked it how long it would take to drive from where I’ll be staying to the DevDay event, and once again, Siri nailed it.</p>
<p id="p103">To bring the conversation with Siri together, I asked Siri to summarize the details in a note, assuming it would pick Apple Notes. I was pleasantly surprised to get a menu of options, including Apple Notes, <a href="https://getdrafts.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Drafts</a>, <a href="https://theuntitled.site/parchment" rel="noopener noreferrer">Parchment</a>, <a href="https://www.finalist.works/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Finalist</a>, and <a href="https://www.overdesigned.net/cheatsheet/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cheatsheet</a>, none of which were open on my Mac. I picked Drafts and Siri created a note. The only disappointment was that I asked Siri to open the note and it said it couldn’t, even though it could and did when I asked a second time.</p>
<p id="p104">That last failure is instructive because Siri isn’t perfect. You’ll hit roadblocks, even when it comes to things that it has done for you before. How much of that is just the nature of LLMs and how much is attributable to Siri’s integration with apps is impossible to tell, but as with any chatbot, it’s important to recognize that Siri AI makes mistakes, too, so proceed with caution and verify what you’re told.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-121817-pm2x-1789230026819-8a7a9d9720.png" alt="Siri can be used for research."><p class="image-caption">Siri can be used for research.</p></div>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/macos-review-drafts-travel-redacted-1789345014262-2273f9fdfb.png" alt="In another test, Siri summarized iPhone information and saved the result in Drafts."><p class="image-caption">In another test, Siri summarized iPhone information and saved the result in Drafts.</p></div>
<p id="p107">Another useful tool is Siri’s ability to pull information from whatever is onscreen. That can be an app like Safari that’s built into Golden Gate or a third-party app. In my testing, onscreen awareness works far better from Spotlight than it does from the standalone Siri app. For example, I’ve been able to gather information from multiple apps and summarize them in Apple Notes with no problem. However, when I tried the same thing with the Siri app, it was often confused by floating panels from apps like <a href="https://dropoverapp.com" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dropover</a>.</p>
<p id="p108">Your experience with asking Siri to perform actions in apps will vary depending on the apps you use. Siri integrates well with Calendar, Notes, Reminders, Mail, Messages, Maps, and Photos, although not perfectly. Venture beyond those apps and the experience depends largely on how their developers expose content to Spotlight and actions to Siri through App Intents.</p>
<p id="p109">Other apps, both from Apple and third-party developers, generally don’t work or only work in limited ways. As I edit this review in Obsidian, Siri is unable to tell me what my most recently edited document is, for example. And, if I ask Siri for the last email I received in <a href="https://www.macstories.net/reviews/spark-mail-adds-a-mac-cli-and-agent-skills/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Spark</a>, it cheats a little, looking the information up in Apple Mail instead. That’s a useful workaround, but it’s still a workaround. If my MacStories email address wasn’t set up in both places, Siri would have been stuck.</p>
<p id="p110">That points out an important caveat about Siri that Apple can’t resolve alone. The ability for it to take actions in apps is limited by the developers who implement it. If you use apps that aren’t supportedf for messaging, email, and calendaring, you’ll miss out on a big part of what Siri can do until they ship support for modern App Intents.</p>
<p id="p111">However, what is in Apple’s control and merits more attention is the breadth and depth of Siri AI support for its own apps. For instance, I just asked Siri to name the most recent file in my Downloads folder, and it picked one from three hours and 18 files and folders ago. In my testing, the Siri app couldn’t rename files, move them, or duplicate them, although Apple Intelligence can suggest names in Finder.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-123239-pm2x-1789230814741-307a9d7e62.png" alt="The most recent file Siri could find in my Downloads folder was from more than three hours and 18 files ago."><p class="image-caption">The most recent file Siri could find in my Downloads folder was from more than three hours and 18 files ago.</p></div>
<p id="p113">The same is true of Apple Music, which needs a lot of work. I asked how many songs I have in my Music library and was told “26.” I have thousands. I asked Siri to play Phoebe Bridgers’ latest album and instead of playing the album in Apple Music, an episode of Switched on Pop began playing, but the Podcasts app didn’t open and the Siri app didn’t show that it was playing something from Apple Podcasts. Once I realized I was listening to a podcast, I opened Apple Podcasts and saw that it was playing an episode about Phoebe Bridgers’ new album. Then I understood what was happening and could stop playback.</p>
<p id="p114">As I explained in my Golden Gate preview, this is frustrating because apps like Finder and Music include lots of useful metadata and work with a variety of automation tools. Some support seems to be available via existing Shortcuts actions, but not all of it, and unlike Shortcuts, the Siri app can’t use the command line or AppleScript as a way to extend what the app can do.</p>
<p id="p115">That said, I’m encouraged by what I’ve seen so far and optimistic about where App Intents is heading. Where Apple supports App Intents in Golden Gate, the experience has improved rapidly as the beta season comes to a close. It’s not perfect, but it’s reliable enough to make it a good solution for many people. Just as encouraging is the growing adoption by third-party developers, especially the indie developers we cover regularly.</p>
<p id="p116">As some of my examples demonstrate, there’s still plenty of room for improvement and expansion for Siri, but what’s out today is worth trying. I think you’ll find that in many cases, Siri will help you work more quickly and efficiently, which I love.</p>
<p id="p117"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h3 id="personal-context">Personal Context</h3>
<p id="p118">The final pillar of Siri AI that I need to cover is personal context, which is Apple’s way of describing the information Siri can access about you when responding to your queries. As with actions, personal context has improved a lot over the summer. For example, just weeks ago, Siri couldn’t identify my immediate family or find the Illinois address I lived at for over 20 years, but now it can.</p>
<p id="p119">However, while it can provide me with a bio of myself and Federico, it often struggles to explain how we know each other. That’s not a problem for other chatbots. After asking Siri how I know Federico in multiple ways across multiple separate conversations, I suspect the problem stems from Siri’s reliance on Contacts to answer questions about people. Coax Siri to investigate more deeply, and you’ll get a more satisfying answer.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-20135-pm2x-1789236842984-5777017ce1.png" alt="At times, Siri struggles with making connections between people that should be obvious."><p class="image-caption">At times, Siri struggles with making connections between people that should be obvious.</p></div>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-21255-pm2x-1789236834228-36f73a9344.png" alt="Eventually, Siri will get there, but it can take some coaxing."><p class="image-caption">Eventually, Siri will get there, but it can take some coaxing.</p></div>
<p id="p122">Personal context draws from sources like your email, text messages, calendar, photos, and notes. In my testing, personal context is best at finding information about me in supported apps. It’s not as strong at combining that information with the context of my request. That doesn’t make personal context any less useful as a search tool, but to me “personal context” connotes a deeper understanding of who you are and your circumstances.</p>
<p id="p123">Here’s what I mean. On Labor Day, I asked Siri to suggest a restaurant I’d like, specifically mentioning that it was Labor Day as a hint that some places would be closed. Siri came up with two spots I like because I had threads in Messages confirming past online orders. It then asked whether I would like to check their hours.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-22516-pm2x-1789237563868-306906a05c.png" alt="Siri used past order confirmation texts to suggest restaurants."><p class="image-caption">Siri used past order confirmation texts to suggest restaurants.</p></div>
<p id="p125">I’m probably expecting too much, but there’s a lot wrong with that response. First, multiple order confirmations in Messages may be a good indicator of places I like, but the scope of the search is too narrow. There are lots of restaurants nearby that I like from which I’ve never placed an online order. However, Siri doesn’t have access to my credit card charges, so it doesn’t know about those places, although it could probably be inferred from driving direction requests and the time I spend at certain places based on Maps data.</p>
<p id="p126">Another issue is that I specifically asked about eating out on Labor Day. Most places near me were closed for the holiday, which was the case for one of the two places Siri suggested. Given my specific mention of Labor Day, that should have been taken into account in the first instance. What I’m getting at is that the personal context I’d like Siri to have (and use securely and privately) is much broader and deeper than a search of Messages, Apple Mail, or other apps.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-22114-pm2x-1789237316721-6cada96973.png" alt="Siri feels like magic when you ask for something like a URL from months ago."><p class="image-caption">Siri feels like magic when you ask for something like a URL from months ago.</p></div>
<p id="p128">That said, personal context feels like magic when you ask it for a URL or address you know is somewhere in Messages, Mail, or Notes, but you don’t know where. It just finds those things without you having to remember where the data lives. That’s genuinely useful and a great addition to Siri. Still, it’s closer to search than what I consider the kind of context where it would feel like Siri “knows” you. Maybe that’s too creepy for a lot of people, but if done privately and securely, that’s where I hope Siri is heading because it would take Siri to an altogether different level.</p>
<hr id="hr129"><p id="p130">A month ago, I wouldn’t have recommended Siri. Today, I would. It’s good for finding personal information, answering everyday questions, and performing tasks in supported apps. Its limitations still appear somewhat unpredictably, but it has become useful enough to earn a place in my day.</p>
<p id="p131"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h2 id="system-level-apple-intelligence-features">System-Level Apple Intelligence Features</h2>
<h3 id="siris-new-expressive-voices-and-improved-dictation">Siri’s New Expressive Voices and Improved Dictation</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-23120-pm2x-1789237970380-0685af759f.png" alt="Siri's new expressive voices are accessible from the Siri section of System Settings."><p class="image-caption">Siri’s new expressive voices are accessible from the Siri section of System Settings.</p></div>
<aside class="info-box " id="aside133"><p>Siri’s new expressive voices and improved dictation both require <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/06/apple-introduces-siri-ai-a-profoundly-more-capable-and-personal-assistant/" rel="noopener noreferrer noopener noreferrer">an M3 or newer Mac with at least 12GB of memory</a>.</p>
<p class="icon"><svg viewbox="0 0 120 120" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle clip-rule="evenodd" cx="60" cy="60" fill-rule="evenodd" r="60"></circle><path d="m44 89v-5h13.2v-28.6h-13.1v-5h19.1v33.6h12.8v5zm10.8-53.1c0-2.8 2.2-4.9 5-4.9s5 2.1 5 4.9-2.2 5-5 5-5-2.1-5-5z" fill="#fff"></path></svg></p>
</aside><p id="p134">You can pick one of multiple expressive voices. For American English there are a total of seven voices, two of which are brand new. Other voices include Australian, British, Indian, Irish, and South African English, and both expressiveness and pace can be adjusted with a slider. The UI for this is in the Siri section of System Settings (which replaces Tahoe’s Apple Intelligence and Siri section) and is nearly identical to what you’ll find on the iPhone or iPad.</p>
<p id="p135">The new voices are excellent, and I appreciate the ability to dial in the pacing and expressiveness. I’ve found that with my iPhone, I prefer less expressiveness since I hear Siri far more via turn-by-turn directions while driving, but I’ve turned it up a little on my Mac. Likewise, I have increased the pacing a little on my Mac, but kept the default on my iPhone. Apple’s defaults are good, but it’s worth playing with the settings to see if a different configuration suits your tastes for different contexts like it did for me.</p>
<p id="p136">That said, I don’t use Siri’s voice features nearly as much with my Mac as I do my iPhone or Apple Watch. That’s because my older M1 Max Mac Studio doesn’t support the voices, and with the MacBook Pro I’ve been using, I’m more likely to be somewhere where I wouldn’t want to speak aloud to my laptop. Perhaps that will change when I update my desktop Mac, but for now, I’m content without the feature.</p>
<p id="p137">Apple has <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/06/apple-introduces-siri-ai-a-profoundly-more-capable-and-personal-assistant/" rel="noopener noreferrer">improved dictation accuracy</a>, too. You won’t see any difference in System Settings or anywhere else. The changes are all under the hood. On supported Macs, dictation will work better automatically. I’ve tried this with my M1 Max Mac Studio, which doesn’t support the new dictation, and an M4 Max MacBook Pro, which does, and the difference is noticeable. Speaking quickly or quietly leads to frequent dictation errors on the M1 Max Mac Studio, but the M4 Max MacBook Pro reproduced my words exactly, making it a good choice for on-device dictation if you have a newer Mac.</p>
<h3 id="finder-file-naming-and-actions">Finder File Naming and Actions</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/macos-review-finder-coordinates-redacted-1789345008456-156ecc05a5.png" alt="A spot-on suggestion for an image of a game controller."><p class="image-caption">A spot-on suggestion for an image of a game controller.</p></div>
<p id="p139">One of the low-key best Siri features of Golden Gate is its ability to suggest file names. If you’ve ever downloaded a PDF of a bill from a website, you probably have a collection of incomprehensible file names filled with seemingly random strings of numbers and letters. With the macOS 27 update, simply highlight the file name and Siri will suggest a couple of names using the content of the file. The feature also works inside document saving dialogs and with folders.</p>
<p id="p140">My experience with this feature has been good, but not perfect. Siri generally picks meaningful short titles similar to what I’d come up with myself, but once in a while it has suggested a calendar event instead if my file has a date in its name, which my screenshots often do. That said, this is a terrific feature I know I’ll use a lot.</p>
<p id="p141">You can also complete a handful of actions on files from Finder, including sending them via Mail or Messages, using either the contextual Ask Siri popup or Services menu. This is effectively the same limitation I covered above in the Siri app section, which is disappointing if you spend a lot of time wrangling files.</p>
<p id="p142"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h3 id="writing-and-editing">Writing and Editing</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-25541-pm2x-1789239384064-3e91009e0d.png" alt="Apple Intelligence underlines spelling and grammar mistakes in blue."><p class="image-caption">Apple Intelligence underlines spelling and grammar mistakes in blue.</p></div>
<p id="p144">Apple’s writing and editing tools have been improved in a variety of ways. Automatic proofreading works well, although I have less use for the drafting and rewriting features. As you type, blue underlining will appear beneath misspellings and grammatical errors, letting you make corrections one at a time.</p>
<p id="p145">On my M1 Max Mac Studio, it can take a few seconds for the blue underlining to appear, but in practice, that delay isn’t problematic. I don’t typically go back to make corrections until I’m at a break point in the writing process.</p>
<p id="p146">What I like most about the proofreading suggestions is that they’re automatic. The blue lines simply appear, allowing me to quickly identify problem areas and step through them one at a time.</p>
<p id="p147">Siri has become a drafter and editor with Golden Gate, too. You can ask it to write something from scratch, adjust its tone, or rewrite something you wrote. While I don’t use AI-generated rewrites in my published work, I do have models create checklists, how-tos, and document workflows for me regularly. Having tried Golden Gate’s new tools to ask about the tone of my writing and do stylistic rewrites, I can report that they work as advertised, but I personally don’t like them. I’ve not seen a rewrite that I think is better than my own writing. However, I have found editing something like how-to instructions for conciseness is helpful.</p>
<p id="p148">Curiously, if you use Siri to change the style of your writing, you’ll get the side-by-side comparison of the original and edited version that Pages offers for proofreading, even if you’re using TextEdit or Notes. Why that comparison shows up for editing and not proofreading in some apps is a curious choice, and something I hope is made consistent in a future update.</p>
<p id="p149">Siri’s writing capabilities go a little further in Mail and Messages, offering <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/06/apple-introduces-siri-ai-a-profoundly-more-capable-and-personal-assistant/" rel="noopener noreferrer">suggested replies in your own voice</a>. I’ve used a lot of apps that offer something similar and have never been comfortable with using them. Apple’s suggestions are on par with other apps I’ve used, but I don’t see myself ever using them.</p>
<p id="p150">On balance, Apple’s Siri writing and editing tools have made great strides from their origins. They are better integrated with the OS and system apps, and the quality of the text and edits they produce is better, too. When the tools were first introduced, I wouldn’t have recommended them to anyone. While I wouldn’t substitute them for a careful human editing pass, they’re useful for catching spelling and grammatical errors before you send an email.</p>
<h3 id="visual-intelligence">Visual Intelligence</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/visual-intelligence-cropped-1789241936117-832be7bd1e.png" alt='Inspecting a Safari page using Visual Intelligence and "Ask Siri."'><p class="image-caption">Inspecting a Safari page using Visual Intelligence and “Ask Siri.”</p></div>
<p id="p152">Apple has also added a keyboard shortcut for Visual Intelligence. Typing ⌘-⇧-Space <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/macos-golden-gate-10-features/" rel="noopener noreferrer">invokes Siri, highlighting whatever visible window is under your pointer</a> along with the Ask Siri contextual popup and a suggested action or two. The feature can be used to summarize or copy the text in an app, identify items in a photo, translate something, explain a chart, and more.</p>
<p id="p153">The Visual Intelligence shortcut is a cool trick but not one that I’ve used much in practice. More often, I grab a URL, screenshot, text, and other data, dump them into Codex and let it do its thing. The Siri app accepts text, images, and files, but it couldn’t retrieve contents webpages I gave it. So if you want to compare two iPhone models, for example, you’re either going to have to rely on Siri’s world knowledge (which is stronger on iPhones than on particular models of running shoes), extract the website content another way, or rely on something like Visual Intelligence.</p>
<h3 id="apple-intelligence-for-automation">Apple Intelligence for Automation</h3>
<p id="p154">Apple has also brought its on-device and PCC models to the command line via the new <a href="https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2026/241/?time=1025" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>fm</code> tool</a>, along with a <a href="https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2026/241/?time=1105" rel="noopener noreferrer">Python library for accessing its on-device model</a>. That makes it easy to incorporate the models into scripts run from Terminal.</p>
<p id="p155">These additions are great to see. There are a lot of automation use cases for Apple’s models, especially when you can process private data entirely on your Mac, so I’m glad Apple has made it easy to build them into your own workflows.</p>
<hr id="hr156"><p id="p157">Apple Intelligence has made great strides this year on the Mac. The difficulty with assessing its capabilities lies in the fact that everyone uses these tools a little differently and any one person’s use case may not work as well as another’s. But having spent the summer with Siri and Apple’s other Intelligence features, I feel like they finally have some momentum. The AI models are capable enough to support the OS features, and they fit more naturally into the fabric of the OS and its apps.</p>
<p id="p158">There’s no denying that Siri is light years ahead of where it was just a year ago. It really is a useful tool that you should try. I think you’ll find that there are myriad little interactions throughout your day that it makes better. That’s quite an achievement by itself, but that’s not the only stick by which Siri should be measured.</p>
<aside class="pull-quote aligncenter" id="aside159"><blockquote>
<p>The AI world doesn’t wait for point releases, and Apple can’t afford to either.</p>
</blockquote>
</aside><p id="p160">Apple doesn’t need to match every capability of <a href="https://chatgpt.com/work/" rel="noopener noreferrer">ChatGPT Work </a>or Claude Cowork any more than Notes needs to match <a href="https://www.notion.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Notion</a>. There’s room for products with different ambitions and audiences. But for everyday questions and tasks, Siri still competes directly with those companies’ free chatbots, and its reliability needs to measure up.</p>
<p id="p161">That’s why I hope Apple continues improving Siri AI as quickly as it did over the summer beta season. The AI world doesn’t wait for point releases, and Apple can’t afford to either. My hope is that PCC continues to improve through server-side updates and that on-device models are updated frequently, too.</p>
<p id="p162">Apple has built a much more stable foundation for its AI features with macOS 27 and its other OSes. That’s an important first step because it will set the stage for years to come. I’m a fan of Apple’s privacy and security approach and the way it has integrated Apple Intelligence more deeply into its OSes. With Golden Gate, Apple has turned a new page on Apple Intelligence, transitioning from a story of missed opportunities to one about everyday utility. What counts now is whether Apple builds on its progress quickly enough for Apple Intelligence to be relevant.</p>
<p id="p163"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h2 id="performance-improvements">Performance Improvements</h2>
<p id="p164">Golden Gate’s performance gains were a big part of Apple’s pitch at WWDC this year. A “<a href="https://512pixels.net/2015/04/the-snow-leopard-moment/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Snow Leopard</a>” release <a href="https://9to5mac.com/2026/06/05/macos-27-could-grant-two-wishes-that-longtime-mac-users-want-most/" rel="noopener noreferrer">had been rumored leading up to Apple’s developer conference</a>, which turned out to be correct. When Federico and I sat down with Apple representatives for a briefing on the improvements along with other press people, it was Apple who mentioned Snow Leopard first. I was surprised, but when I got my hands on Golden Gate for the first time, I immediately understood why Apple leaned into the Snow Leopard vibes. The release really is faster.</p>
<p id="p165">This is going to be the shortest section of this review. Not because Golden Gate’s performance improvements aren’t important, but because my assessment is based on everyday use rather than benchmarks. Over the summer, I’ve noticed improvements across both my M1 Max Mac Studio and the M4 Max MacBook Pro Apple loaned me for testing.</p>
<p id="p166">Faster speeds have a tendency to start feeling like the new normal very quickly. You get used to them, and before long, it’s hard to tell whether you simply imagined the increases in the first place.</p>
<p id="p167">That’s not been entirely true with macOS 27. In fact, AirDrop still feels like magic compared to prior versions. I AirDrop screenshots to my Mac almost daily, and a multi-image send was often painfully slow, taking me out of the flow of putting my screenshots together for a story. Now, it’s so fast that it may as well be instantaneous in most instances, whether I’m sending images from my iPhone, iPad, or another Mac, all of which were on the betas this summer.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-43645-pm2x-1789245518143-c153b2b818.png" alt="Browsing folders on my Mac mini server over Ethernet feels nearly as fast as browsing local folders."><p class="image-caption">Browsing folders on my Mac mini server over Ethernet feels nearly as fast as browsing local folders.</p></div>
<p id="p169">Another area that is much faster is browsing networked storage. I’ve hung SSDs and hard drives off of an Ethernet-connected Mac mini in another part of my house. Regardless of the speed of the drive, listing the contents of a large folder across the network can be slow. That’s not been the case with Golden Gate. Accessing the files on my Mac mini server over Ethernet has been smooth and fast all summer long.</p>
<p id="p170"><a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/macos-27-golden-gate-the-macstories-public-beta-preview/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apps launch faster under Golden Gate</a>, too. Not every app launches instantly, especially those built on web technologies, but with native apps, the difference is real and noticeable.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-42054-pm2x-1789244723349-ec2e3419c2.png" alt="Spotlight brings together relevant results from across my Mac."><p class="image-caption">Spotlight brings together relevant results from across my Mac.</p></div>
<p id="p172">Other noticeable improvements include Spotlight indexing. After installing new betas throughout the summer, I often saw Spotlight indexing the next time I opened it, but it always finished quickly. Just as important, searching that index is much faster, too, with results displaying as quickly as I can type. That was not the case at all with some point releases of Tahoe where Spotlight got so badly bogged down that I went back to third-party launchers for a while.</p>
<p id="p173">In addition to speedier searches, Apple <a href="https://www.apple.com/ae/newsroom/2026/08/apple-introduces-mac-studio-with-m5-max-and-m5-ultra/" rel="noopener noreferrer">rebuilt the search foundation</a> used by Spotlight, Photos, and Mail to ensure it’s more up to date and complete. Mail also adds <a href="https://www.apple.com/os/macos/" rel="noopener noreferrer">a new ranking system</a> that surfaces relevant emails in “Top Results,” while Spotlight offers more relevant search suggestions.</p>
<p id="p174">The same sort of search speed is found in Messages and Mail. Federico is handling the other new features of those apps in <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-27-review/" rel="noopener noreferrer">his iOS and iPadOS 27 review</a>, but their search capabilities are worth reiterating here. There’s more to good search than speed, but it goes a long way, and with Golden Gate, I’ve had no trouble finding past messages and emails when searching in Messages and Apple Mail. Both apps have been quick and reliable, although I’m currently using <a href="https://sparkmailapp.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Spark Mail</a> more often than Apple Mail.</p>
<p id="p175">Scrolling and animations have felt smoother, too, although I notice the difference less now than when I first installed Golden Gate.</p>
<p id="p176">And that leads me to something that I think is important about these performance improvements to macOS 27. We’re living through <a href="https://www.idc.com/resource-center/blog/global-memory-shortage-crisis-market-analysis-and-the-potential-impact-on-the-smartphone-and-pc-markets-in-2026/" rel="noopener noreferrer">dramatic price increases in memory and storage fueled by the AI data center boom</a>. That makes improvements that help us get more out of the Macs we already own especially valuable. If an update makes an older Mac feel faster, it makes keeping it another year more appealing. With Golden Gate, Apple has breathed new life into your Mac, making it work more smoothly whether it’s a first-generation Apple silicon machine or a brand-new model. The company’s timing couldn’t be better, and it should be commended for helping users squeeze more out of every Mac it sells.</p>
<p id="p177">I also have a bit of advice for everyone who updates to Golden Gate. Bask in the glory of faster app launches, AirDrop transfers, and all the rest. As pronounced as the initial speedups felt when I first installed macOS Golden Gate, most became the new normal very fast, so appreciate them while you can.</p>
<p id="p178"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h2 id="apps">Apps</h2>
<p id="p179">Many of this year’s app updates span Apple’s platforms, so Federico and I have divided the coverage between our reviews. <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-27-review/" rel="noopener noreferrer">His iOS and iPadOS 27 review</a> covers:</p>
<ul id="ul180"><li>Calendar</li>
<li>Camera</li>
<li>Clock</li>
<li>Files</li>
<li>Find My</li>
<li>Journal</li>
<li>Mail</li>
<li>Messages</li>
<li>Music</li>
<li>Notes</li>
<li>Phone</li>
<li>Photos</li>
<li>Reminders</li>
<li>Safari</li>
<li>Shortcuts</li>
</ul><p id="p181">For my part, I’m covering:</p>
<ul id="ul182"><li>iPhone Mirroring</li>
<li>Sidecar</li>
<li>Preview</li>
<li>Image Playground</li>
<li>Weather </li>
<li>Maps</li>
<li>Podcasts</li>
<li>Home</li>
<li>the Mac-only features of Notes and Freeform</li>
<li>FaceTime</li>
</ul><p id="p183">Before getting into the apps, two features announced at WWDC won’t be available at launch: automatic upgrades for weak or compromised passwords and suggested actions in Messages conversations.</p>
<h3 id="iphone-mirroring">iPhone Mirroring</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-45742-pm2x-1789246739009-3593d8a404.png" alt="Reminders running wide in iPhone Mirroring."><p class="image-caption">Reminders running wide in iPhone Mirroring.</p></div>
<p id="p185">I’m a big fan of iPhone Mirroring. Until Nintendo released a web app, I used iPhone Mirroring almost weekly to listen to <a href="https://www.nintendo.com/us/online/nintendo-switch-online/nintendo-music/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Nintendo Music</a> on my Mac. But it’s become more than that. With iPhone notifications turned on so they appear on my Mac, it’s become an old habit to click on a notification, which opens iPhone Mirroring directly to the app that sent the message. I give it a quick check and then move on.</p>
<p id="p186">With macOS 27, iPhone Mirroring now supports <a href="https://developer.apple.com/macos/whats-new/" rel="noopener noreferrer">adaptive layouts</a>. That means you can resize the iPhone Mirroring window to many more sizes than were possible before. The connection to the iPhone Duo <a href="https://www.macstories.net/news/iphone-18-pro-and-iphone-duo-the-macstories-overview/" rel="noopener noreferrer">announced just last week</a> is difficult to miss. Also, resizing apps like Weather and Reminders gives developers something of a preview of how Apple’s apps adapt to the iPhone Duo’s wider screen.</p>
<p id="p187">That said, it’s not a one-to-one comparison. Resizing a mirrored app doesn’t reproduce the Duo’s interface. <a href="https://www.macstories.net/news/iphone-18-pro-and-iphone-duo-the-macstories-overview/" rel="noopener noreferrer">At the Surprise and Shine Event</a>, Apple also showed controls positioned along the sides of the Duo’s screen for easier thumb access.</p>
<p id="p188">Still, seeing widescreen versions of Apple’s iPhone apps over the summer was a fun exercise in imagining what apps on a folding iPhone would look like. Now, with the hardware revealed, it’s merely a more convenient way to experience your iPhone apps on a Mac. And why not? You have a big screen at your disposal, and adaptive layouts let iPhone apps that support them make better use of that display. I plan to use iPhone Mirroring even more as a result.</p>
<aside class="info-box " id="aside189"><p>You can get to Control Center in iPhone Mirroring now via the View menu or ⌘4.</p>
<p class="icon"><svg viewbox="0 0 120 120" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle clip-rule="evenodd" cx="60" cy="60" fill-rule="evenodd" r="60"></circle><path d="m44 89v-5h13.2v-28.6h-13.1v-5h19.1v33.6h12.8v5zm10.8-53.1c0-2.8 2.2-4.9 5-4.9s5 2.1 5 4.9-2.2 5-5 5-5-2.1-5-5z" fill="#fff"></path></svg></p>
</aside><p id="p190">You can also play DRM-protected video via iPhone Mirroring. It’s not likely to work with every video service, but it’s a step in the right direction. Pretty nifty.</p>
<h3 id="sidecar">Sidecar</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/img-1309-2-1789254473654-1ade911c0f.gif" alt="Interacting with Safari using touch via Sidecar."><p class="image-caption">Interacting with Safari using touch via Sidecar.</p></div>
<p id="p192">If ever there were an app that screamed “we’re working on a touchscreen Mac!” it’s Sidecar. Sidecar has been a great way to use an iPad as a second screen for your Mac for years now, but it hasn’t seen updates very often since <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/macos-catalina-the-macstories-review/3/#sidecar-and-window-management" rel="noopener noreferrer">its Catalina introduction</a>.</p>
<p id="p193">This year is different because Sidecar now supports touch, letting you interact with your Mac’s interface with your fingers. And you know what? It’s not hard to do. The Mac has smaller UI targets than an iPhone or iPad, but those targets have been getting bigger and the UI more spread out for years. It started with Big Sur, <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/macos-big-sur-the-macstories-review/7/" rel="noopener noreferrer">as I noted in my review</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote194"><p>
  Whitespace is used by Big Sur to make information more glanceable, and windows feel more expansive too.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p195">Tahoe had multiple examples of the trend, too, including <a href="https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2025/310/?time=670" rel="noopener noreferrer">taller buttons</a>. macOS 26 also made buttons easier to identify without hovering, which also benefits touch, as I pointed out <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/macos-26-tahoe-the-macstories-review/2/" rel="noopener noreferrer">in that review</a>:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote196"><p>
  Button groupings are always visible in Tahoe, whereas they only appear on hover in Sequoia.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p197">Sidecar also gives users a chance to discover which Mac interactions work well with touch. And while I wouldn’t use touch for everything in Sidecar, scrolling and poking at buttons and menus is a lot easier than has been <a href="https://www.macworld.com/article/1471888/the-mac-isnt-ready-for-a-touchscreen.html" rel="noopener noreferrer">imagined in the past</a>. I love that I can scroll a webpage with one finger, tap a Finder sidebar item, and choose toolbar commands without using my trackpad or mouse. So whether Sidecar portends touchscreen Macs or merely makes it easier to interact via a device that was designed for touch from the get-go, I’m here for it.</p>
<h3 id="preview">Preview</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/10/screenshot-2026-09-09-at-92715-pm-1789003701884-b6bd8cbc93.png" alt="Editing a USDZ file of the iPhone Duo in Preview."><p class="image-caption">Editing a USDZ file of the iPhone Duo in Preview.</p></div>
<p id="p199">I didn’t go into WWDC with “Preview becomes a 3D editing tool” on my bingo card, but here we are. On the surface, it may seem curious, but Preview’s never been about just PDFs. It’s always been capable of editing a wide variety of images. Viewing 3D content isn’t new either, but this year <a href="https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2026/285/" rel="noopener noreferrer">macOS Golden Gate adds 3D editing</a>.</p>
<p id="p200">The app allows users to manipulate objects, edit properties and lighting, explore scene hierarchies, and convert or compress assets. Rendering options include Apple’s own <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/realitykit" rel="noopener noreferrer">RealityKit</a>, <a href="https://openusd.org/release/api/hd_storm_page_front.html" rel="noopener noreferrer">Storm</a>, and a new raytracer, all with support for <a href="https://academysoftwarefoundation.github.io/OpenPBR/" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenPBR</a>, a standard for describing how materials look and respond to light.</p>
<p id="p201">I’m so used to using Preview to do simple things like resize or crop an image or delete a page from a PDF that I was blown away by the depth of what Preview can do with 3D assets. This isn’t the sort of functionality that I expect to have a use for anytime soon, but I’m glad it’s there. It’s part of macOS and gives tinkerers a way to experiment with 3D assets without first learning a dedicated editing app. Making 3D tools more accessible could also encourage more people to experiment with assets for Apple Vision Pro. That’s pretty cool.</p>
<h3 id="image-playground">Image Playground</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/image-playground-artwork-comparison-1789252148730-66e81ff226.png" alt="Images generated by Image Playground in Tahoe using ChatGPT (left) and Golden Gate using Apple's Foundation Models (right)."><p class="image-caption">Images generated by Image Playground in Tahoe using ChatGPT (left) and Golden Gate using Apple’s Foundation Models (right).</p></div>
<p id="p203">I’m not a fan of generative image tools for a host of reasons, and even if I were, Apple’s haven’t been very good to date. Image Playground is substantially better than before, but even its new photorealistic images still look off to me. Generated images of people in my life have an uncanny valley feeling. I can tell who the image is meant to depict, but it’s only a rough approximation of them.</p>
<p id="p204">macOS 27’s update is about more than people, though. Image generation now runs on Private Cloud Compute, and the app offers more control over the results. You can preserve the parts of an image you like while replacing individual elements or choosing a different aspect ratio, for example.</p>
<p id="p205">In the name of science, I took Image Playground’s new generation and editing tools for a spin. While I’m still not a fan of the images it makes, they are undeniably more realistic. I’ve also been able to remove objects and change their colors reliably, which makes natural language editing more useful than before. I don’t see myself using Image Playground, but it’s there if you want it.</p>
<p id="p206">Also, <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/06/apple-intelligence-brings-powerful-ai-capabilities-into-everyday-experiences/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apple is using SynthID</a>, which was <a href="https://deepmind.google/models/synthid/" rel="noopener noreferrer">developed by Google DeepMind</a>, to watermark images created with Image Playground, so they can be identified as AI-generated.</p>
<p id="p207"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h3 id="weather">Weather</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/14/macos-review-weather-addresses-redacted-1789345001540-fe81bf381f.png" alt="Weather in Tahoe (top) and Golden Gate (bottom)."><p class="image-caption">Weather in Tahoe (top) and Golden Gate (bottom).</p></div>
<p id="p209">Weather has been redesigned for Golden Gate with a more orderly presentation. In Tahoe, the app featured an hourly forecast that stretched across the entire window (unless there was precipitation) just beneath the current conditions. Much of the more detailed weather data was confined to square tiles. Together it all formed an even rectangle, but the large number of square tiles made the design look a little messy, which also made it harder to scan quickly.</p>
<p id="p210">With macOS 27, the details below the current conditions are broken into two columns. The left column features a series of square and rectangular tiles, while the right column is bigger, allowing for more expansive layouts for hourly and daily forecasts and radar, which need the additional space. The bottom of both columns is anchored by a single row of square tiles. The underlying weather data hasn’t noticeably changed, but the new layout makes it easier to scan.</p>
<p id="p211">Two other changes are worth noting. There is now a segmented control near the top of the window to switch between current conditions, forecast precipitation, and wind. When each is selected, the hourly and daily forecasts change, as does the radar map. It’s an excellent addition that works equally well on every platform. Apple has also added a new Highlights section to Weather that appears at the top of the app’s weather data and summarizes notable upcoming weather events. Highlights is currently available only in U.S. English.</p>
<h3 id="maps">Maps</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/10/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-73729-am2x-1789040703621-9e846c2d48.png" alt="Enhanced Flyover."><p class="image-caption">Enhanced Flyover.</p></div>
<p id="p213">Maps’ two marquee features are <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/06/apple-unveils-innovative-features-and-intelligence-experiences-across-services/" rel="noopener noreferrer">enhanced Flyover mode and Local Lists</a>. With Flyover, certain cities, including Boston, New York, London, and Chicago, include more detailed 3D renderings of their skylines. Turn on 3D mode and zoom in, and you’ll see what I mean. Apple has enhanced the 3D models for these cities with the help of AI to compose more detailed and realistic cityscapes.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/10/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-74615-am2x-1789040852777-02c919ff69.png" alt="A Local List of Manhattan attractions."><p class="image-caption">A Local List of Manhattan attractions.</p></div>
<p id="p215">Available in the U.S., Local Lists are a little like Guides, except instead of being hand-curated, they’re generated based on spots that are trending with Apple Maps users, like the list of Manhattan landmarks in my screenshot. The lists update periodically, too.</p>
<h3 id="other-app-changes">Other App Changes</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/10/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-101551-am2x-1789049799204-93a599735f.png" alt="Searching from See All Episodes makes finding classic podcast episodes easier than ever."><p class="image-caption">Searching from See All Episodes makes finding classic podcast episodes easier than ever.</p></div>
<p id="p217"><strong>Podcasts:</strong> Apple Podcasts on the Mac is catching up with the video features available on iOS, iPadOS, and visionOS. You can switch between watching and listening to a podcast on your Mac when a show supports <a href="https://podcasters.apple.com/video-apple-podcasts" rel="noopener noreferrer">HLS video streaming</a>. Mac users can view the same video using Picture in Picture and can <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-au/guide/podcasts/pod53101237/mac" rel="noopener noreferrer">download it for offline viewing</a>, which is great when traveling, too. Also, every Apple platform gains the ability to <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/06/apple-unveils-innovative-features-and-intelligence-experiences-across-services/" rel="noopener noreferrer">search from a podcast’s See All Episodes view</a>, making finding back catalog episodes of a show easier.</p>
<p id="p218"><strong>Home:</strong> Home adds tools for finding and understanding HomeKit Secure Video recordings to all compatible Apple platforms, including the Mac. The new features require <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/macos-release-notes/macos-27-release-notes#HomeKit" rel="noopener noreferrer">an iCloud+ plan with at least 2TB of storage</a>, a compatible home hub such as an Apple TV 4K or second-generation HomePod, and an Apple Intelligence-capable iPhone, with Apple Intelligence enabled in Home.</p>
<p id="p219">Apple uses <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/macos-release-notes/macos-27-release-notes#HomeKit" rel="noopener noreferrer">on-device processing and Private Cloud Compute</a> to describe recorded footage and collect recordings of the same event from multiple cameras. You can also search for clips by the video’s description.</p>
<p id="p220">The vast majority of my clips are simply described as “Someone is seen,” which isn’t very helpful as a description or for searching later. Admittedly, those recordings are an unexciting collection of trips in and out of our garage and taking out the trash. However, when it comes to security cameras, mundane footage is exactly what I hope to capture. The question is whether Home can provide useful detail when something out of the ordinary happens, which my testing hasn’t yet answered.</p>
<h3 id="notes-and-freeform">Notes and Freeform</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-10-at-105443-am2x-1789252817611-f9f8742658.png" alt="Drawing on the Magic Trackpad in Notes."><p class="image-caption">Drawing on the Magic Trackpad in Notes.</p></div>
<p id="p222">Federico covered Notes in <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/ios-and-ipados-27-review/" rel="noopener noreferrer">his iOS and iPadOS 27 review</a>, but there’s one small addition to Notes on the Mac worth highlighting: drawing with your finger on a Magic Trackpad or using a mouse. Just select the drawing tool and start scribbling by clicking and dragging on the trackpad or holding down the mouse button and dragging. Drawing with your finger isn’t easy, but Notes does a nice job of smoothing out your squiggles, and I can see this coming in handy as a way to quickly create a diagram or sign your name and paste it into a PDF. The same drawing feature has been brought to <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/freeform/id6443742539" rel="noopener noreferrer">Freeform</a>, too.</p>
<p id="p223">It’s worth noting that this feature works with touch and the mouse only. You can’t draw on the Magic Trackpad using an Apple Pencil or third-party iPad stylus like the <a href="https://amzn.to/4gTy4dG" rel="noopener noreferrer">Logitech Crayon</a>.</p>
<h3 id="facetime">FaceTime</h3>
<p id="p224">This year, Apple is <a href="https://www.apple.com/os/macos/" rel="noopener noreferrer">expanding Communication Safety</a> to help protect children from images and videos depicting gore and violence. The protection extends to FaceTime on the Mac and iPhone. Apple also says users will enjoy improved FaceTime call quality on poor connections, although I haven’t been able to verify whether those improvements extend to the Mac. Mac users can also select <a href="https://www.apple.com/macos/feature-availability/#accessibility-live-captions" rel="noopener noreferrer">Mandarin Chinese (Taiwan) for Live Captions</a>.</p>
<p id="p225"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h2 id="everything-else">Everything Else</h2>
<p id="p226">There are a bunch of other little updates sprinkled throughout macOS 27 Golden Gate.</p>
<h3 id="accessibility">Accessibility</h3>
<p id="p227"><a href="https://www.macstories.net/news/apple-intelligence-infused-accessibility-features-promise-greater-flexibility-and-power/" rel="noopener noreferrer">As I wrote in May</a>, Apple is expanding accessibility in macOS 27, including several features powered by Apple Intelligence. Among the features I covered then are:</p>
<ul id="ul228"><li>VoiceOver image descriptions that allow Image Explorer to describe photos, scanned documents, and onscreen images in greater detail than ever. </li>
<li>Accessibility Reader, which better handles complex documents, including multi-column layouts with images and tables, and enables summaries and translation.</li>
<li>Generated video subtitles for everything from video taken with your iPhone to streaming services.</li>
<li>Better pairing reliability for MFi hearing aids with handoff between devices and a better macOS setup experience.</li>
<li>Support for <a href="https://www.playstation.com/en-us/accessories/access-controller/" rel="noopener noreferrer">the Sony Access controller</a>.</li>
</ul><h3 id="external-displays">External Displays</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-72307-pm2x-1789255418656-aba6d25239.png" alt="My Mac Studio can run my ASUS display at 120Hz."><p class="image-caption">My Mac Studio can run my ASUS display at 120Hz.</p></div>
<p id="p230">For those who use multiple displays and non-Apple displays, there’s good news in Golden Gate. macOS now <a href="https://www.apple.com/os/macos/" rel="noopener noreferrer">restores window positions and Spaces</a> and remembers which screen they belong to better than before. This is especially handy when connecting and disconnecting a laptop. I’m a one-display-at-a-time kind of guy, so I haven’t used this feature a lot, but in my limited testing it worked as described.</p>
<p id="p231">macOS now supports more resolutions and refresh rates than before. I can now run <a href="https://amzn.to/3Gx0agy" rel="noopener noreferrer">my 32-inch ASUS display</a> at 4K and 120Hz. HDR can be turned on for photos, video, and Golden Gate’s UI, too.</p>
<h3 id="everyday-conveniences">Everyday Conveniences</h3>
<p id="p232">There’s a new <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/macos-release-notes/macos-27-release-notes" rel="noopener noreferrer">keyboard shortcut</a> (⌘⇧F) to access Recents in Open and Save dialogs. In addition, <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/macos-release-notes/macos-27-release-notes" rel="noopener noreferrer">Move and Resize controls</a> now work with Open and Save dialogs that are independent windows.</p>
<p id="p233">Also, pull to refresh has been added to Safari, Mail, News, and Calendar and is triggered with a downward swipe on your trackpad. It’s another interaction that would feel right at home on a touchscreen Mac.</p>
<h3 id="user-control">User Control</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/untitled-18-1789255842646-e6a6d1131b.png" alt="An open Claude and backgrounded ChatGPT."><p class="image-caption">An open Claude and backgrounded ChatGPT.</p></div>
<p id="p235">The Dock gets a change in macOS 27, too. If you quit an app and it is still <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appkit/managing-ongoing-background-processes-in-your-mac" rel="noopener noreferrer">running a background process</a>, the indicator beneath its Dock icon turns to light gray. You can stop that activity by right-clicking on the app’s icon and picking “Stop Running in Background.” If you repeatedly stop an app’s background activity, Golden Gate will offer to prevent it from running in the background in the future.</p>
<p id="p236">I’m a little torn over this feature. On the one hand, I appreciate knowing which apps may still be using resources after I quit them and like the control this offers. Yet keeping those apps’ icons in the Dock adds clutter, and I don’t think most people will understand what the light gray indicator means. I know I didn’t at first, and I expect that some users might think something nefarious is happening in the background, which isn’t necessarily the case.</p>
<p id="p237">macOS 27 adds extensive new Screen Time controls for parents, including Time Allowances for app categories and Schedules for app access during certain times of the day. Ask to Browse lets kids ask permission to visit websites, and there are expanded protections against gore and violence, alongside existing nudity protections. If you have young children, these features are worth a closer look. My kids are all grown, so I haven’t had real-world experience with these controls myself. It’s also worth noting that the new Screen Time features <a href="https://www.apple.com/child-safety/" rel="noopener noreferrer">require all devices in your Family Sharing group to be on Apple’s latest series of OSes</a>.</p>
<h3 id="moving-on">Moving On</h3>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/codex-image-sep-12-2026-07-40-00-pm-1789256432313-012650677e.png" alt="Listing what's left of my Intel-based apps."><p class="image-caption">Listing what’s left of my Intel-based apps.</p></div>
<p id="p239">macOS 27 Golden Gate <a href="https://www.apple.com/os/macos/" rel="noopener noreferrer">can’t run on Intel-based Macs</a>. However, Intel apps <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/102527" rel="noopener noreferrer">can still run through Rosetta</a>. With Golden Gate, you can visit System Settings → General → About → Intel-based Apps → Details to view the list of your Intel-based apps. I had a few old Intel-based apps that I have since removed from my Mac. Apple has also retired AirPort Utility. If you already have it installed, the app won’t disappear, but it’s <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/macos-release-notes/macos-27-release-notes#AirPort-Utility" rel="noopener noreferrer">no longer bundled with clean installs</a> of macOS. Finally, if you want to get ready for next year, encrypted HFS+ volumes will <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/125615" rel="noopener noreferrer">no longer be supported</a> starting with macOS 28.</p>
<p id="p240"><!--nextpage--></p>
<h2 id="one-story-ends-and-another-begins">One Story Ends and Another Begins</h2>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/09/12/cleanshot-2026-09-12-at-75656-pm2x-1789257490798-d4ae63f6e7.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p242">When I wrote <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/macos-26-tahoe-the-macstories-review/" rel="noopener noreferrer">my macOS 26 Tahoe review last year</a>, I felt like I’d just left the beach for the summer with a book that was promising but that I hadn’t had time to finish. Tahoe added a bunch of great productivity features, but Apple Intelligence was barely out of the gate, and Liquid Glass felt unfinished.</p>
<p id="p243">Fast forward one summer, and the vibe is different. This summer, macOS 27 Golden Gate gave Apple the chance to tie up loose ends, delivering a satisfying conclusion to the book I’d started the year before by refining Liquid Glass, improving OS performance, and making Apple Intelligence feel more at home on the Mac. But Golden Gate also feels like the beginning of another story, arriving alongside <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/04/tim-cook-to-become-apple-executive-chairman-john-ternus-to-become-apple-ceo/" rel="noopener noreferrer">a change in Apple’s leadership</a>.</p>
<p id="p244">John Ternus is often described as <a href="https://www.apple.com/leadership/john-ternus/" rel="noopener noreferrer">a hardware guy</a>, and that tracks with his career at Apple. But during the Surprise and Shine event, what stood out to me was his emphasis on what hardware enables. Introducing <a href="https://www.macstories.net/news/iphone-18-pro-and-iphone-duo-the-macstories-overview/" rel="noopener noreferrer">iPhone Duo</a>, he explained that Apple started with the experience it wanted to deliver and worked toward the hardware that could make it possible. That’s an encouraging perspective for someone overseeing the future of the Mac.</p>
<p id="p245">Apple’s leadership transition comes at a chaotic moment in tech that will shape how people use the Mac. Golden Gate’s priorities are clear. The update makes existing hardware more useful, gives users more control over background activity, and integrates AI in a way that honors the company’s privacy philosophy.</p>
<aside class="pull-quote aligncenter" id="aside246"><blockquote>
<p>As an opening chapter, Golden Gate is a good one. I’m hooked. I want to see what comes next.</p>
</blockquote>
</aside><p id="p247">What encourages me is that Apple has begun translating its approach to AI into useful everyday features. Keeping control of my data matters, but so does having useful tools. Golden Gate brings both closer together.</p>
<p id="p248">Make no mistake, Apple has a long way to go. It’s not clear at all that this story will have a happy ending for Apple or any other tech company. Apple’s emphasis on privacy and security should be celebrated, but it doesn’t resolve the broader questions AI raises about society, the environment, and our culture.</p>
<p id="p249">Siri still needs to be more reliable, and I’d like the Siri app to do more, but I’m rooting for Apple on this one. As much as I get out of tools like Codex and Claude Code for building automations and apps and ChatGPT Work and Claude Cowork for running MacStories with Federico, there’s still a lot of data I’d prefer to process on my Mac or through <a href="https://security.apple.com/blog/private-cloud-compute/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Private Cloud Compute</a>.</p>
<p id="p250">As an opening chapter, Golden Gate is a good one. I’m hooked. I want to see what comes next. There are hints of good things to come, from a more capable Siri to touchscreen Macs and other gadgets. It’s going to be messy and chaotic, and I’m eager to see what comes of it.</p>
<p id="p251">2027 is going to be a page-turner.</p>
